{
 "qid": "Q28",
 "names": [
  "Hungarian Republic"
 ],
 "title": "Hungarian Republic",
 "variantOf": "Q28",
 "endonym": {
  "native": "Magyar Királyság",
  "roman": "Magyar Királyság"
 },
 "capsule": "After the short-lived Hungarian Soviet Republic collapsed in August 1919, counter-revolutionary forces under Admiral Miklós Horthy took power, and on 1 March 1920 the National Assembly restored the Kingdom of Hungary as a monarchy without a monarch, with Horthy serving as regent. Horthy's regime pursued revisionist foreign policy to recover territory lost by the 1920 Treaty of Trianon, allying with Nazi Germany to reannex parts of Czechoslovakia, Romania, and Yugoslavia between 1938 and 1941, before the Axis alliance ended in German occupation in March 1944 and Horthy's forced removal in October 1944, when the fascist Arrow Cross Party seized power.",
 "citations": [
  {
   "label": "Britannica: Hungary",
   "url": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Hungary"
  },
  {
   "label": "Wikipedia: Miklós Horthy",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mikl%C3%B3s_Horthy"
  }
 ],
 "edited": "era-variant",
 "attestation": "Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The territory of interwar Hungary is precisely defined by the Treaty of Trianon and the Vienna Awards, with boundary lines documented in international treaty instruments; the dataset label 'Hungarian Republic' is a misnomer for the Kingdom of Hungary under regency. Capsule researched for this polity/era specifically (confidence: medium).",
 "attestationLinks": [
